  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-ETH154</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved institutional capacities of constituents to promote gender equality and extend social protection in Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia has made progress in establishing social protection frameworks, but coverage remains low, particularly for informal workers, women, and vulnerable groups. Key challenges include weak coordination, limited technical capacity, financing gaps, and insufficient integration of gender equality and care-related policies into social protection and labour systems. 
CPO ETH154 addresses these challenges by strengthening the capacity of government, workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations to apply international labour standards on social protection and gender equality through social dialogue. It supports improved coordination, sustainable financing, and expansion of social security coverage, while promoting gender-responsive measures such as equal pay, maternity protection, and care solutions. The programme also aims to extend social protection to vulnerable groups, including informal workers, refugees, and host communities. 
As a result, policies, laws, and workplace practices are expected to better reflect international labour standards, leading to more inclusive, coordinated, and sustainable social protection systems. Coverage and access to benefits will expand, while care-responsive measures will improve conditions for women and workers with family responsibilities. Stronger data systems and institutional capacity will enhance governance and evidence-based decision-making. The programme benefits government institutions, workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations, and workers in both formal and informal sectors, especially women and those with care responsibilities. Indirect beneficiaries include vulnerable groups such as low-income households, people with disabilities, refugees, and host communities, who will gain from expanded coverage and improved social protection services.</narrative>
  </description>
